---
/**
 * foodcraft / HeroBL — "Kalinaw Coffee"'s arrival.
 *
 * ROLE. The page's opening statement and its only h1. Two equal columns on a
 * cream ground: on the left a rust eyebrow, a Fraunces headline whose closing
 * phrase falls into ITALIC RUST, a lede, two actions and a row of outlined
 * pills; on the right one tall ARCHED photograph. Lifted verbatim from the
 * element carrying data-component="HERO" in "Kalinaw Coffee Landing.dc.html":
 * section padding 58px 32px 0, grid minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr) at 52px with
 * align-items:center, h1 clamp(44px,5.9vw,86px)/1.02 at 19ch with margin
 * .2em 0 .28em, lede 17.5px/1.72 at 44ch with 32px beneath it, actions 12px
 * apart, pills 10px apart 26px below the actions, and a 520px-tall plate at
 * 200px 200px 14px 14px.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every path is declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts, so each is live in the sidebar AND inline):
 *   hero.image          — data-image-field, the ARCHED photograph
 *   hero.kicker         — the eyebrow (.kick)
 *   hero.headline       — the h1, set:html so an owner <em> takes the rust italic
 *   hero.headlineLines.${i} — the SAME h1, when hero.headline holds nothing but
 *                         the derived business name; see THE HEADLINE below
 *   hero.sub            — the lede
 *   hero.cta1.text + data-href-field hero.cta1.href — the rust button
 *   hero.cta2.text + data-href-field hero.cta2.href — the outlined button
 *   hero.meta1          — pill 1
 *   hero.meta2          — pill 2
 *   hero.trustLine      — pill 3
 *
 * WHICH PATH THE THREE PILLS BIND TO, and why it is not trust.cells. The design
 * feeds this row from its own `heroTags` list at hint-placeholder-count="3".
 * The brief offered trust.cells.${i}.label as a last resort; it is a worse fit
 * for a reason worth writing down. trust.cells is FOUR num+label pairs, and
 * TrustBL renders all four as the espresso band immediately below this section
 * — so binding the pills to those labels prints the same three phrases twice,
 * a hand's width apart, and an owner editing one pill silently rewrites the
 * band under it. The schema instead declares three short, hero-scoped, single
 * strings that no other BL section has an element for: hero.meta1, hero.meta2
 * and hero.trustLine. Three declared fields for three pills, each its own
 * stable scalar path — so R6 cannot even arise here, since there is no list and
 * no index to drift. HeroBI/BJ/BK all noted these three as "schema-owned but
 * this design has no element for them"; this design does.
 * FOR REVIEW: the sidebar hint on hero.trustLine still reads "Used by
 * Stillwater hero only." That sentence is now inaccurate — it is a one-line
 * schema edit, and it is not this file's to make. Flagged, not done.
 * NOTE ON meta1's PLACEHOLDER: the schema shows '★★★★★ rated on Google' as
 * placeholder text. A placeholder is not a default — lib/derive-content-
 * defaults.ts deliberately emits NO meta1 (the comment there explains that the
 * old default asserted a five-star score for businesses with no reviews). This
 * file renders the owner's typed value or no pill at all, and prints no star
 * glyph of its own anywhere.
 *
 * DERIVED, never stored and never editable:
 *   · the photograph's accessible name — layout.businessName and nothing else
 *   · both button hrefs, resolved through onPage() against the ids PageBL
 *     actually renders
 *   · which of the two headline paths the h1 binds to
 *   · the italic rust tail, when the owner has not marked one (see THE
 *     SIGNATURE)
 *   · the flat variant (no photograph ⇒ one column), below
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · No copy of any kind is written by this file. The design's demo strings
 *     ("Slow mornings, strong coffee.", "See the menu", "Coffee house · Cebu",
 *     "Roasted on site") are the DESIGNER filling in their own template. A real
 *     café gets an arrival that says only what that café said, so every guard
 *     sits OUTSIDE its tag: no eyebrow over nothing, no empty action row, no
 *     empty pill, and no arched cream slab standing in for a photograph nobody
 *     uploaded.
 *   · hero.tag / hero.photos.* — not declared by the schema. A data-field on a
 *     path no sidebar input owns looks editable and focuses nothing.
 *   · No star row of any kind. The design has none here, and a star drawn from
 *     anything other than a numeric rating field is a fabricated claim.
 *   · .reveal. This section is above the fold; it is painted, not animated in.
 *   · An aria-label on the <section>. Naming it would make it a landmark
 *     announced with a word I wrote ("Hero"); the h1 already titles the region.
 *   · target="_blank" on an off-page CTA. The design's demo markup carries it
 *     because its primary is hard-wired to a Messenger URL; here the href is
 *     the owner's and may be an in-page anchor, so the family idiom (BJ, BK)
 *     of leaving navigation alone is kept.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE, and why one heading reads two paths. This design's headline is
 * ONE flowing sentence whose closing phrase falls into rust italic — not a
 * stack of lines — so the h1 is hero.headline rendered through set:html and the
 * emphasis is an <em> the OWNER types.
 * The catch is WHICH FIELD actually holds the owner's headline, because two
 * layers upstream disagree:
 *   · lib/derive-content-defaults.ts auto-fills hero.headline with the BARE
 *     BUSINESS NAME on every named site (`headline: name || undefined`), and
 *     lib/astro-builder.ts's mergeShallow lets that derived value stand
 *     wherever the owner left the field unset.
 *   · lib/services/groq.service.ts — the extraction that writes the copy for a
 *     real submission — emits hero.headlineLines and NEVER hero.headline.
 * So "always prefer hero.headline" prints the business name and drops the
 * authored headline on the floor for exactly the sites that have one. The rule
 * is therefore: hero.headline wins whenever it is AUTHORED, meaning anything
 * other than the business name the derive layer fills in. When it is that
 * auto-fill (or empty), the surviving headlineLines rows win — flowed INLINE
 * inside the same h1, because this design's headline is a sentence and not a
 * stack — each carrying its ORIGINAL array index, so dropping a blank row 2
 * cannot misdirect an edit into row 3 (R6).
 * The field a click opens is always the field the visible text came from:
 * binding hero.headlineLines.0 to text that arrived as hero.headline (or the
 * reverse) aims an inline edit at a slot the sidebar is not showing.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). The arrival renders when it has something to SAY or SHOW:
 * a headline, a lede, or the photograph. It deliberately does NOT count the
 * eyebrow (derive-content-defaults builds one from city + trade for sites with
 * neither name, tagline nor upload), does NOT count the two buttons (derive
 * sets cta1/cta2 text UNCONDITIONALLY, so counting either makes the gate a
 * no-op that always passes), and does NOT count the pills (hero.meta2 derives
 * to the bare city, which would let a site with nothing but a location open on
 * a page containing one chip).
 * FOR REVIEW: this is the one section in the family whose gate counts the
 * headline, because here the headline IS the body — it is the document's only
 * h1, and a hero dropped for having "only" an h1 takes the page's heading with
 * it and opens the site on the trust band.
 *
 * NO PHOTOGRAPH ⇒ ONE COLUMN. The plate is a COLUMN here, not a background. If
 * the owner uploaded nothing, rendering it anyway leaves an arched cream slab
 * beside the copy — a hole with a fancy top. So the plate is dropped entirely
 * and the grid collapses to a single track; the h1's 19ch and the lede's 44ch
 * keep the copy at the same measure it had in the left column, so the text is
 * not re-flowed to fill a width the design never gave it.
 *
 * THE SECTION HAS NO BOTTOM PADDING, and that is the design, not an oversight.
 * The HERO element ends at `padding:58px 32px 0`; the espresso TRUST band below
 * carries its own 62px of margin, and every other section in the family brings
 * its own top padding from the wrapper's `section` rule. Adding a floor here
 * would double it.
 *
 * THE ARCH is the ONE literal length in this file (R1's stated exception):
 * 200px 200px on the top corners, --r-md on the bottom two. It is a shape, not
 * a colour. It is left at a flat 200px in the narrow queries on purpose — when
 * the plate is narrower than 400px the browser scales both top radii down
 * proportionally, which turns the arch into a true semicircle rather than
 * breaking it. That is the right small-screen form of the same shape.
 *
 * COLOUR / CONTRAST (R1 — no literal hex in this file, only PageBL's tokens):
 *   --ink headline on --paper 14.6:1 · --ink-soft lede 6.71:1 · --accent
 *   eyebrow and italic 5.31:1 · --ink-soft pill label 6.71:1 inside a --line
 *   hairline · --on-accent on the rust fill 5.31:1. Every one of these is the
 *   design's own pairing, taken from the wrapper's token table unchanged.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME LAW (not inferable, do not "tidy"): the section is .kc-arrive, NOT
 * .hero — lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts force-paints `.hero .btn-ghost` with
 * !important and would strand a label here. Buttons are .btn + .btn--fill /
 * .btn--line (double dash) for the same reason. Never .btn-primary /
 * .btn-ghost / .btn-yellow / .btn-light / .nav-cta / .trust / .testi /
 * .cta-band in this family.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;

// Anchor guard, copied from the BK sibling and re-pointed at THIS page.
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes '#visit' for hero.cta1 and
// '#services' for hero.cta2 — neither is an id this family renders, so both
// buttons scrolled nowhere. Resolve every in-page href against what PageBL
// actually draws: keep it when the id exists, remap it when this design carries
// the same block under another id, otherwise fall back to the target the design
// was built around. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http) pass through untouched.
// Keep PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS in step with PageBL — and with the copies
// in HeaderBL, CtaBandBL and FooterBL. There is no shared import by design (a
// section component in this repo receives only `content` + `layout`); the
// copies must stay identical by hand.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'location', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  visit: 'location', contact: 'location',
  services: 'menu', rooms: 'menu',
  host: 'about', why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
  book: 'main',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[href.slice(1)];
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(href.slice(1))) return href;
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const hero = content.hero || {};

// Display fallbacks only — the HOOK below is always hero.image, the path the
// sidebar's image picker writes. The quote escape keeps a filename with an
// apostrophe from closing the url('') early and killing the declaration.
const heroImage = String(hero.image || (hero.photos && hero.photos[0]) || (content.photos && content.photos[0]) || '');
const heroImageCss = heroImage.replace(/'/g, '%27');

const kicker = hero.kicker || '';

// THE HEADLINE — full reasoning in the doc comment. hero.headline is this
// design's field; the rows take over only when hero.headline is nothing but the
// business name derive-content-defaults auto-filled (or is empty), which is the
// shape every AI-written submission arrives in.
const bizName = String(layout?.businessName ?? '').trim();
const headline = String(hero.headline ?? '').trim();
const lineRows: Array<{ text: string; i: number }> = (Array.isArray(hero.headlineLines) ? hero.headlineLines : [])
  .map((t: any, i: number) => ({ text: String(t ?? ''), i }))
  .filter((l: { text: string }) => l.text.trim() !== '');
// …and only when they SAY something hero.headline does not. On the ordinary
// named site both hold the same one line, and the single-line hook keeps it.
const useRows =
  lineRows.length > 0 &&
  (headline === '' || headline === bizName) &&
  lineRows.map((l) => l.text.trim()).join(' ') !== headline;
const hasHeadline = useRows || headline !== '';

// ── THE SIGNATURE, MADE REACHABLE ────────────────────────────────────────
// This design's h1 is two-tone: ink, then a rust italic tail ("Slow mornings,
// strong coffee."). It got there because the designer typed <em> into their own
// markup. No owner is going to do that from a text box, so without this the
// headline renders one flat colour on every real site and the most recognisable
// move in the template never ships.
//
// When the owner has NOT marked emphasis themselves, the tail is derived from
// their OWN sentence and adds not one word:
//   • more than one line                  -> the last line
//   • a comma, dash, colon or semicolon   -> everything after the LAST one
//   • anything else                       -> nothing at all
//
// That last case is deliberate. A single clause is a single clause, and
// splitting inside one would lean on a word the owner never leaned on — the
// design's own headline breaks at a comma precisely because that is where its
// sentence turns.
//
// An explicit <em> always wins: this only fills a silence.
const hasOwnEm = (v: string): boolean => /<em[\s>]/i.test(v);
const emphasise = (raw: string): string => {
  if (!raw || hasOwnEm(raw)) return raw;
  const lines = raw.split(/\r?\n/).map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
  if (lines.length > 1) {
    const tail = lines.pop() as string;
    return lines.join(' ') + ' <em>' + tail + '</em>';
  }
  // The LAST break wins, so a sentence with two commas emphasises its close
  // rather than its middle. The lookahead keeps a trailing comma from matching.
  const re = /[,;:—–]\s+(?=\S)/g;
  let cut = -1;
  let m: RegExpExecArray | null;
  while ((m = re.exec(raw)) !== null) cut = m.index + m[0].length;
  if (cut <= 0 || cut >= raw.length) return raw;
  return raw.slice(0, cut) + '<em>' + raw.slice(cut) + '</em>';
};
const headlineHtml = emphasise(headline);
// The rows path marks its LAST row instead, and only when no row is already
// carrying the owner's own <em>.
const rowsOwnEm = lineRows.some((l) => hasOwnEm(l.text));

const sub = hero.sub || '';
const cta1Text = hero.cta1?.text || '';
// The design's primary is its contact action and its secondary points at the
// menu; those are the two fallbacks when an owner's href resolves to nothing.
const cta1Href = onPage(hero.cta1?.href, '#location');
const cta2Text = hero.cta2?.text || '';
const cta2Href = onPage(hero.cta2?.href, '#menu');

// The three pills. Three declared scalar paths, not a list — see the doc
// comment for why this is not trust.cells. Each is guarded on its own, and the
// row itself disappears when all three are empty.
const tag1 = hero.meta1 || '';
const tag2 = hero.meta2 || '';
const tag3 = hero.trustLine || '';
const hasTags = !!(tag1 || tag2 || tag3);

// The photograph's accessible name is the owner's own words or nothing. With no
// business name there is no honest name for the picture, so it leaves the
// accessibility tree instead of announcing a phrase I invented. Built as ONE
// spread object so a conditional attribute can never end up beside a literal
// (R9 — two BK components shipped exactly that duplication).
const photoA11y: Record<string, string> = bizName
  ? { role: 'img', 'aria-label': bizName }
  : { 'aria-hidden': 'true' };

const hasArrival = !!(hasHeadline || sub || heroImage);
---
{hasArrival && (
<section class={heroImage ? 'kc-arrive' : 'kc-arrive kc-arrive--flat'}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kc-grid">
      <div class="kc-copy">
        {kicker && <span class="kick kc-kick" data-field="hero.kicker">{kicker}</span>}
        {!useRows && headline !== '' && <h1 class="kc-h1" data-field="hero.headline" set:html={headlineHtml}></h1>}
        {useRows && (
          <h1 class="kc-h1">
            {lineRows.map((l, k) => (
              <span class:list={['kc-ln', !rowsOwnEm && k === lineRows.length - 1 && 'kc-ln--em']}><span data-field={`hero.headlineLines.${l.i}`} set:html={l.text}></span>{k === lineRows.length - 1 ? '' : ' '}</span>
            ))}
          </h1>
        )}
        {sub && <p class="lede kc-lede" data-field="hero.sub">{sub}</p>}
        {(cta1Text || cta2Text) && (
          <div class="kc-ctas">
            {cta1Text && (
              <a class="btn btn--fill" href={cta1Href} data-field="hero.cta1.text" data-href-field="hero.cta1.href">{cta1Text}</a>
            )}
            {cta2Text && (
              <a class="btn btn--line kc-cta-line" href={cta2Href} data-field="hero.cta2.text" data-href-field="hero.cta2.href">{cta2Text}</a>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
        {hasTags && (
          <div class="kc-tags">
            {tag1 && <span class="chip" data-field="hero.meta1">{tag1}</span>}
            {tag2 && <span class="chip" data-field="hero.meta2">{tag2}</span>}
            {tag3 && <span class="chip" data-field="hero.trustLine">{tag3}</span>}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>

      {/* The arched photograph. No upload ⇒ no column at all, rather than an
          arched cream slab standing in for a picture nobody supplied. */}
      {heroImage && (
        <div class="plate kc-shot">
          <div
            class="ph kc-ph"
            data-image-field="hero.image"
            style={`background-image:url('${heroImageCss}');`}
            {...photoA11y}
          ></div>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from PageBL's token vocabulary
     (--paper --well --ink --ink-soft --accent --line --disp --sans --r-md) and
     from its shared primitives (.wrap .kick .lede .chip .btn .btn--fill
     .btn--line .plate .ph). There is no literal hex in this file; the arch
     radius is the one literal length, and it is a shape.

     HOW THESE RULES WIN, and the one config that would break them. Astro scopes
     a style block with the project's `scopedStyleStrategy`, and this project
     takes the default, 'attribute' — so `.kc-shot` ships as
     `.kc-shot[data-astro-cid-…]`, specificity (0,2,0). The wrapper's primitives
     are `html[data-page="BL"] :where(.plate)`, specificity (0,1,1), because
     :where() contributes nothing. (0,2,0) beats (0,1,1), which is how the arch
     replaces the plate's --r-md corner, the h1 keeps its .2em margins against
     the shared `margin:0`, the lede keeps its 32px floor, and none of it needs
     !important.
     Setting scopedStyleStrategy:'where' in astro.config.mjs would collapse
     every one of those to (0,1,0) and silently hand the wrapper the win — the
     arch would quietly become a 14px rectangle. Two-class rules
     (`.kc-ctas .btn`) survive that; the single-class ones above do not. */

  /* Design: max-width:1240px; margin:0 auto; padding:58px 32px 0. The measure
     and the gutter are .wrap's job; the 58px top and the ZERO floor are this
     section's. See the doc comment on why there is no bottom padding. */
  .kc-arrive {
    padding: 58px 0 0;
  }

  /* Design: grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr); gap:52px;
     align-items:center. */
  .kc-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 52px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* No photograph ⇒ one track. The copy keeps its own 19ch / 44ch measures, so
     it is not re-flowed to a width the design never gave it. */
  .kc-arrive--flat .kc-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  /* min-width:0 lets a long word wrap inside the track instead of widening it
     and pushing the page sideways. */
  .kc-copy { min-width: 0; }

  /* Design: font-size:clamp(44px,5.9vw,86px); line-height:1.02;
     margin:.2em 0 .28em; max-width:19ch. Larger and tighter than the shared
     .h2, which is why this is an h1 rule of its own and not that primitive. */
  .kc-h1 {
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(44px, 5.9vw, 86px);
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    margin: .2em 0 .28em;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 19ch;
  }
  /* THE SIGNATURE. Fraunces ships a real italic at 500, so this is not a
     synthesised oblique. :global() because Astro cannot scope markup that
     arrived through set:html (R3). */
  .kc-h1 :global(em) { font-style: italic; font-weight: 500; color: var(--accent); }
  /* Rows path only (hero.headlineLines). They FLOW as one sentence rather than
     stacking, because this design's h1 is a sentence — the wrap comes from the
     19ch measure, exactly as it does on the single-line path. The word space
     between rows is a real text node in the markup, not a faked margin. */
  .kc-ln { display: inline; }
  /* The derived tail on the rows path. Same treatment as an owner-typed <em>,
     declared separately because the rows are real elements this file owns and
     do not need :global(). */
  .kc-ln--em { font-style: italic; font-weight: 500; color: var(--accent); }

  /* Design: the lede's 17.5px/1.72/44ch is the shared .lede verbatim (its clamp
     tops out at exactly that size); only the 32px beneath it is local. */
  .kc-lede { margin: 0 0 32px; }

  /* Design: display:flex; gap:12px; flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:center. */
  .kc-ctas {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* The design's outlined secondary is the shared button at 30px of side
     padding rather than 32px — the only metric in which it differs. */
  .kc-cta-line { padding-left: 30px; padding-right: 30px; }

  /* Design: display:flex; gap:10px; flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top:26px. The pills
     themselves are the shared .chip, whose 10px/.14em/7px 14px/--line hairline
     already match this design exactly. */
  .kc-tags {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 26px;
  }

  /* Design: height:520px; border-radius:200px 200px 14px 14px; overflow:hidden;
     and the design's plate colour, which is --well and arrives with the .plate
     primitive. The wrapper's .plate reserves a 180px minimum for short plates;
     this one states its own height, so that floor is released. */
  .kc-shot {
    height: 520px;
    min-height: 0;
    border-radius: 200px 200px var(--r-md) var(--r-md);
  }
  /* Two classes deep on purpose: the wrapper's `:where(.plate) .ph` scores one
     element more than a single scoped class would, so `.kc-ph` alone would lose
     to it. Restating cover/center costs nothing and means the photograph cannot
     letterbox if that primitive is ever narrowed. No colour is restated — that
     stays in the primitive, where the theme engine keeps control of it. */
  .kc-shot .kc-ph {
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  /* ── R8 · this section's OWN grid. The wrapper handles the shared 760/520
     pass; the two columns are this file's problem and collapse here. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .kc-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 36px;
    }
    /* The FLOOR is the design's own minimum — the low end of its own
       clamp(44px,5.9vw,86px). At 38px it sat below the cap in the 520 query
       below, so the headline GREW from 38 to 44px as the screen got narrower;
       44px here meets that cap exactly and the ramp only ever descends. */
    .kc-h1 { font-size: clamp(44px, 7vw, 64px); max-width: 22ch; }
    .kc-lede { margin-bottom: 28px; }
    /* Stacked, the plate is now full width, so 520px of it would be a wall
       between the copy and the band below. */
    .kc-shot { height: clamp(360px, 52vw, 460px); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    /* Nothing may push the page sideways at 360px: long unbroken strings break
       anywhere, and a long uppercase label wraps inside a full-width button
       instead of widening the row. */
    .kc-arrive { padding-top: 44px; }
    .kc-grid { gap: 30px; }
    .kc-kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .kc-h1 { font-size: clamp(31px, 9vw, 44px); max-width: none; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .kc-lede { max-width: none; overflow-wrap: anywhere; margin-bottom: 24px; }
    .kc-ctas { gap: 10px; }
    .kc-ctas .btn { flex: 1 1 100%; white-space: normal; text-align: center; }
    /* Re-join the wrapper's 520 button padding: without this the class above
       keeps the secondary at 30px while the primary drops to 24px. */
    .kc-cta-line { padding-left: 24px; padding-right: 24px; }
    .kc-tags { gap: 8px; margin-top: 22px; }
    /* A pill carrying a full sentence wraps inside itself rather than running
       off the screen. */
    .kc-tags .chip { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    /* The arch is left at a flat 200px on purpose — see the doc comment. The
       CAP meets the 360px floor of the query above rather than clearing it: at
       380px the photograph got 20px TALLER as the viewport crossed 520px
       downward, which is a plate growing on a smaller screen. */
    .kc-shot { height: clamp(300px, 78vw, 360px); }
  }
</style>
